</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>Results of the comparative experiments assessing the impact of varying <math id="A6.T8.m58" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> for lexical/readability (<math id="A6.T8.m59" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{lex/read}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>lex/read</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{lex/read}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and syntactic (<math id="A6.T8.m60" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{syn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{syn}}</annotation></semantics></math>) features. The ’Readability’, ’Lexical’, and ’Syntactic’ rows show the averaged normalized scores for their respective feature categories. Each group (1, 2, 3) compares a configuration (A) against its inverse (B)</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Effect of Varying <math id="A7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> for Lexical and Syntactic Features</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While employing a uniform <math id="A7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> for all features during inference yields strong performance, our approach allows for the granular adjustment of <math id="A7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> for different feature groups. This flexibility enables a more nuanced control over the model’s behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The readability scores are composite metrics consisting of two components: a word complexity component (calculated based on the number of syllables or letters in words) and a sentence length component. An examination of the underlying formulas for the Flesch Reading Ease, Flesch-Kincaid Grade Level, and Coleman-Liau Index reveals that the lexical component is the predominant factor in the final score and the Gunning Fog Index shows a more balanced contribution of the lexical component and syntactic component. Therefore, when considering our full suite of readability metrics, the collective influence of the lexical component is substantially greater than that of the syntactic component. Consequently, in the following experiments, we group readability features with lexical features.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the impact of assigning different <math id="A7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> values to the “lexical/readability” category versus the “syntactic” category, we designed three comparative experiments. The specific configurations for each experiment are detailed as following.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Group 1</span>. We compare the setting of <math id="A7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{lex/read}}=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>lex/read</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{lex/read}}=0.2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{syn}}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{syn}}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> (Group1-A) against the inverse configuration of <math id="A7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{lex/read}}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>lex/read</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{lex/read}}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{syn}}=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{syn}}=0.2</annotation></semantics></math> (Group1-B).</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Group 2</span>. We compare the setting of <math id="A7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{lex/read}}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>lex/read</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{lex/read}}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{syn}}=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{syn}}=0.8</annotation></semantics></math> (Group2-A) against the inverse configuration of <math id="A7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{lex/read}}=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>lex/read</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{lex/read}}=0.8</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{syn}}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{syn}}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> (Group2-B).</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Group 3</span>. We compare the setting of <math id="A7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{lex/read}}=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>lex/read</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{lex/read}}=0.2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A7.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{syn}}=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{syn}}=0.8</annotation></semantics></math> (Group3-A) against the inverse configuration of <math id="A7.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{lex/read}}=0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>lex/read</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{lex/read}}=0.8</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A7.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{syn}}=0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{syn}}=0.2</annotation></semantics></math> (Group3-B).</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A7.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{lex/read}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>lex/read</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{lex/read}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the value assigned to the lexical and readability features, while <math id="A7.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{syn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{syn}}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the syntactic features.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p8.1" class="ltx_p">As the results shown in Table <a href="#A6.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Appendix F CEFR Sentence Annotator ‣ Controlling Language Difficulty in Dialogues with Linguistic Features" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, our method’s ability to assign different <math id="A7.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> values to feature categories provides a robust and predictable mechanism for fine-grained text attribute control. This flexibility is a significant advantage, enabling users to precisely tune model outputs to meet diverse requirements, such as generating text with simple vocabulary but complex sentence structures, or vice versa, while maintaining high overall quality. An interesting trend is observed in that for the B settings (<math id="A7.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{lex/read}}&gt;t_{\text{syn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>lex/read</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{lex/read}}&gt;t_{\text{syn}}</annotation></semantics></math>), the RSR is lower than for the A settings (<math id="A7.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{lex/read}}&lt;t_{\text{syn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>lex/read</mtext></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>syn</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{lex/read}}&lt;t_{\text{syn}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
